@ifnottex
@node Top
@top Ymacs
This manual describes the Ymacs-specific additions: durable buffers,
profiles, the perpetual session, the store layout, and Info mode. For
everything unchanged from GNU Emacs, read the vendored Emacs manual
(@file{docs/emacs-manual/emacs/}) — chapters here only cover where
Ymacs deliberately differs or extends, and every extension is also
recorded in the divergence ledger
(@file{docs/emacs-manual/divergences.org}).
@end ifnottex

@menu
* Durable Buffers::      Every buffer is a file or a store row.
* Profiles::             Chrome-style tab sets for your whole editor.
* The Perpetual Session:: ymacs never ends by accident.
* The Store::            What lives under ~/.yggterm/ymacs.
* Info Mode::            Reading this manual inside ymacs.
@end menu

@node Durable Buffers
@chapter Durable Buffers

@cindex durable buffers
@cindex unnamed buffers
@cindex scratch, durability of
GNU Emacs keeps unnamed buffers (@samp{*scratch*}) in memory only: kill
the process at the wrong moment and the work is gone. Ymacs replaces
that law outright.

@strong{Every Ymacs buffer is either a file or a row in the store.}
There is no third state and no memory-only buffer.

@itemize @bullet
@item
A buffer visiting a file is saved to that file, exactly as in Emacs
(@pxref{Save Commands,,, emacs, The Emacs Manual}). Its crash-safety
draft lives in the store's @code{drafts} table, not in loose files.
@item
A buffer with no file (scratchpads, captured output, the palette's
internal state) is a row in the @code{buffers} table of the live
profile's store. The write happens at creation and at every command —
@kbd{C-x C-s} is unnecessary for unnamed buffers because @emph{the
write is the save}.
@end itemize

The durability choke point is the command layer: every command's
effect on the current buffer is in the store before the next
keystroke is read. That is what makes macros replay headless and what
makes work impossible to lose.

@node Profiles
@chapter Profiles

@cindex profiles
@cindex sidebar/profiles
Ymacs has @dfn{profiles}, in the sense of a web browser's tab sets
(@pxref{Profiles,,, ychrome}): a named window over a set of buffers.
The profile named @samp{default} exists from first boot.

@table @kbd
@item M-x sidebar/profiles @key{RET}
Open the Profiles sidebar: every profile with the live one marked, and
a field to create a new one. Selecting a row switches profile.
@item M-x ymacs-switch-profile @key{RET} @var{name} @key{RET}
Switch profile by name.
@end table

Switching a profile persists the current profile's open buffer set,
empties the window, and loads the target's — the daemon never stops,
no buffer is deleted, and the other profile's buffers are untouched.
Buffers of one profile are never the business of another.

@strong{ymacs is perpetual.} A GUI restart, a profile switch, an ssh
drop — none of them end the daemon and none of them lose work. The one
way ymacs ends is explicit:

@table @kbd
@item M-x save-buffers-kill-ymacs @key{RET}
Persist every buffer and the session, then stop the daemon.
@end table

@node The Perpetual Session
@chapter The Perpetual Session

@cindex perpetual session
@cindex session restore
The daemon is the durable half of ymacs (the emacsclient model).
Clients — terminal rows, GUI surfaces, headless verbs — come and go;
the daemon holds buffers, the store, and the control endpoint. On any
boot the session is restored from the store: the open set and the
active buffer, per profile. A @file{session.json} from before the
store existed is imported once and then retired.

@node The Store
@chapter The Store

@cindex store layout
@cindex ~/.yggterm/ymacs
Everything ymacs keeps lives under one directory:

@example
~/.yggterm/ymacs/
  state.sqlite3      The store (buffers, drafts, session, profiles).
  control-url        The live daemon's control endpoint.
  daemon.log         The daemon's log.
  user.org           Machine-level settings overrides.
  profiles/
    default/         Per-profile directory (created on first use).
    work/            ...one directory per profile.
  packages/          Package caches (ELPA-style archives land here).
  manual.org snapshot is installed to ~/.local/share/ymacs/manual.org.
@end example

The base @file{~/.yggterm/ymacs/} directory caches packages and holds
the essentials that are machine-wide. Each profile directory holds
that profile's own overrides; @file{profiles/@var{name}/init.lisp} is
evaluated after the shipped @file{init.lisp} defaults, in the same
layering spirit as @file{user.org} for settings (@pxref{Init File}).
An init file that does not exist simply contributes nothing.

@node Info Mode
@chapter Info Mode

@cindex Info mode
@cindex manual, reading
Ymacs ships an Info reader for the forked manual. The sources live in
@file{docs/emacs-manual/ymacs/}; @command{makeinfo} builds
@file{ymacs.info}, and the installation script puts the built manual
where the daemon finds it.

@table @kbd
@item M-x info @key{RET}
Open the Ymacs manual.
@end table

Inside the manual buffer: @kbd{n} next node, @kbd{p} previous node,
@kbd{u} up, @kbd{RET} follow the menu/reference under point,
@kbd{l} back in the history, @kbd{q} quit the manual window. The
reader follows the Emacs Info interface so the fingers transfer; where
it deliberately diverges, the divergence ledger says so.

@bye
